    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          An ultrasonic humidifier is the quietest, cheapest-to-run way to add moisture to a room, because a vibrating plate makes the mist instead of a heater or a big fan. The catch is that it puts whatever is dissolved in your water into the air as well. Buy one with a sensor, feed it distilled or demineralized water if your tap water is hard, clean it twice a week, and it is the best humidifier type for most bedrooms and living rooms. Skip those steps and it is the type most likely to coat your room in white dust.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="how-we-chose" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How We Chose
          </h2>

          <p>
            Ultrasonic humidifiers are the most crowded corner of the humidifier market. Search any retailer and you will find hundreds of near-identical units under brand names that appear and vanish within a year. We started by cutting that list down to brands with a real US presence, a support site and replacement parts you can still buy: Levoit, Dreo, Boneco, Vicks and Pure Enrichment. That alone removed most of the noise, because a humidifier you cannot get a replacement tank or cartridge for is a humidifier you will throw away.
          </p>

          <p>
            Next we required a published tank size, a runtime figure and either a mist output or a room-size rating from the maker or a major retailer. Units that publish nothing useful were dropped. We gave extra weight to a built-in humidity sensor with an auto mode, because an ultrasonic unit without one simply runs until the tank is empty, and that is how a small bedroom ends up with wet windows and a damp mattress. Four of our six picks have a sensor; the two that do not are the cheapest and are labeled honestly.
          </p>

          <p>
            We also looked hard at cleaning. An ultrasonic humidifier sends tiny droplets of tank water straight into the air, so a dirty tank is not a cosmetic problem. Top-fill designs with wide openings scored better than units with narrow bottle-style tanks you cannot get a hand into. We noted where reviewers and owners commonly report fiddly parts, because a humidifier that is a chore to clean is a humidifier that does not get cleaned.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, we kept this list strictly ultrasonic. Several of the picks offer a warm-mist mode, which heats the water before the ultrasonic plate turns it to mist, and that counts. Evaporative wick humidifiers and steam vaporizers do not. If you are still deciding between the two main technologies, our <a href="/evaporative-vs-ultrasonic-humidifier/">evaporative vs ultrasonic humidifier</a> comparison lays out the trade-offs, and our main <a href="/best-humidifier/">best humidifier</a> guide covers every type side by side.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="how-ultrasonic-works" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. How Ultrasonic Humidifiers Work
          </h2>

          <p>
            Inside every ultrasonic humidifier is a small ceramic or metal disc, called a transducer or nebulizer, that vibrates at an ultrasonic frequency under a thin layer of water. The vibration breaks the surface of the water into droplets fine enough to hang in the air as a visible cool mist, and a small fan pushes that mist out of the nozzle. Vicks describes its CoolRelief model as using a built-in nebulizer that quietly vibrates the water, which is a fair one-line summary of the whole category.
          </p>

          <p>
            That design is why ultrasonic units are so quiet and so cheap to run. There is no boiling element, and the fan only needs to move mist, not pull air through a wet wick. Dreo lists its HM713S at 26 watts in cool-mist mode, and Pure Enrichment-class mini units are commonly listed around 12 watts. Compare that with a steam vaporizer that boils water continuously and draws hundreds of watts, and the running-cost argument for ultrasonic is obvious.
          </p>

          <p>
            The same design explains the two big drawbacks. First, nothing in the process separates water from what is dissolved in it. Calcium, magnesium and other minerals in tap water leave with the droplets, dry in the air and settle as fine white dust. Second, anything growing in the tank can also leave with the droplets. The EPA's guidance on home humidifiers notes that ultrasonic and impeller units can disperse both minerals and microorganisms from their tanks into indoor air, which is why the water and cleaning sections below matter more than any spec on the box.
          </p>

          <p>
            Warm-mist ultrasonic units add a heating element that warms the water before it reaches the transducer. Levoit lists its LV600S at 280 watts with the heater on, and Dreo lists the same figure for the HM713S. You get a slightly warmer, often higher-output mist, at roughly ten times the power draw of cool mode. It is an ultrasonic humidifier with a kettle bolted on, not a steam vaporizer, so the white-dust rules still apply.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Maker-Listed Figures</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">26 W</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Dreo HM713S, cool mist</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">280 W</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">LV600S and HM713S, warm mist</p>
              </div>
